\title{TransSynPALM: transfer learning with synthetic phenotype assisted linear mixed models}
\description{
TransSynPALM tests genetic association in a target population, typically an
under-represented ancestry, whose phenotype is measured in only some
participants. It borrows strength from two places. Unphenotyped target
participants contribute through a synthetic phenotype. The source population
contributes through a pooled fit that is decorrelated from the target-only
fit.
}
\details{
A typical analysis has three steps.
\enumerate{
\item \code{\link[=transsynpalm_null]{transsynpalm_null()}} fits the target-only and pooled Syn-PALM null models
once.
\item \code{\link[=transsynpalm_scan]{transsynpalm_scan()}} scores genotype chunks against them and returns
\code{p_tar} (target-only Syn-PALM) and \code{p_de} (decorrelated pooled) for every
variant. \code{\link[=transsynpalm]{transsynpalm()}} runs steps 1 and 2 in one call.
\item \code{\link[=transsynpalm_test]{transsynpalm_test()}} combines \code{p_tar} and \code{p_de} from the whole genome
under the four-group composite null and returns the TransSynPALM p-value.
}

The Syn-PALM fits themselves come from the
\href{https://github.com/haoyu-yang001/SynPALM}{SynPALM} package.
}
